	affiliations = {Helsingin Yliopisto, Faculty of Education, Helsinki, Finland; University of Jyväskylä, Department of Education, Jyvaskyla, Finland},
	abstract = {This longitudinal study examines pre-service language teachers’ perceptions of learning and teaching and how they change during their pedagogical studies. The initial data collected at the beginning of the pedagogical studies consist of visualisations of ideal learning situations and metaphors related to teaching produced by the participants (N = 63). The second data collection took place at the end of the pedagogical studies when the participants returned to reflect on their initial outputs in writing. In this study, we are interested in the kind of narrative of change the participants produce in their reflective texts and what kind of objects of change and change agents they highlight in relation to their pedagogical perceptions. The data have been analysed using thematic content analysis, inspired by a dialogical approach to textual analysis. The results show that changes in perceptions are typically described through consensus talk and supplementary/clarification talk. The objects of change focus on learner-centred orientation, concrete pedagogical knowledge and skills and on the negotiation between ideal and practice. Our analysis indicates that visualisations and metaphors and their written reflections produce nuanced narrative of change, and that the initial outputs often serve as a concrete reference point to illustrate the changes in pedagogical perceptions. © 2025 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.},
