	pages = {},
	doi = {10.3390/mti6120105},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85144719436&doi=10.3390%2Fmti6120105&partnerID=40&md5=1d7663064bcb956942c84a861cfac99a},
	affiliations = {Universidade de Aveiro, Department of Communication and Art, Aveiro, Portugal},
	abstract = {The following study addresses, from a design perspective, narrative visualization using augmented reality (AR) in real physical spaces, and specifically in spaces with no semantic relation with the represented data. We intend to identify the aspects augmented reality adds, as narrative possibilities, to data visualization. Particularly, we seek to identify the aspects augmented reality introduces regarding the three dimensions of narrative visualization—view, focus and sequence. For this purpose, we adopted a comparative analysis of a set of fifty case studies, specifically, narrative visualizations using augmented reality from a journalistic scope, where narrative is a key feature. Despite the strong explanatory character that characterizes the set of analyzed cases, which sometimes limits the user’s agency, there is a strong interactive factor. It was found that augmented reality can expand the narrative possibilities in the three dimensions mentioned—view, focus and sequence—but especially regarding visual strategies where simulation plays an essential role. As a visual strategy, simulation can provide the context for communication or be the object of communication itself, as a replica. © 2022 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.},
	author_keywords = {augmented reality; data visualization; design; exploration; immersive journalism; interactivity; narrative paradox; narrative visualization; storytelling},
